  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: SQL_GROUP_BY
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: SQL_GROUP_BY
  language: 3
  conceptTitle: 'GROUP BY: Grouping Rows'
  symbol: GROUP
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - SQL_09_GROUP_BY
  - HZ_SQL_06_GROUP_BY
  topics:
  - GROUP BY
  definition: GROUP BY puts rows that have the same value in a column into groups,
    so you can work something out for each group.
  howItWorks: 'Write GROUP BY and the column to group by. It is usually used with
    COUNT(*): the result has one row per group, showing the group''s value and how
    many rows it has.'
  exampleCode: "Table: pets\n id | name  | animal | age\n  1 | Bolt  | Dog    |  
    4\n  2 | Misty | Cat    |   2\n  3 | Rex   | Dog    |   7\n\nSELECT animal, COUNT(*)
    FROM pets\nGROUP BY animal;"
  exampleExplanation: 'The rows are grouped by animal: Dog (Bolt and Rex) and Cat
    (Misty). The result has two rows: Dog 2 and Cat 1.'
  tip: GROUP BY makes groups; ORDER BY only sorts. They do different jobs.
